      <h1 class="title">the pursuits of satiya+reed</h1>

      <p>Satiya shouts “Look!”</p>
      <p>And so Reed did just that as she froze mannequin-motionless on the sidewalk squinting at the subject of Satiya’s shoutout only a few steps before her.</p>
      <p>Unfortunate. thought Reed.</p>
      <p>The atmospheric bubble I’d been carefully constructing has now popped, and the residue is too thinly-spread for immediate repair.</p>
      <p>Satiya’s shout interrupted Reed’s ramble which, depending on her audience, could have been taken for disorderly or textured. It was on the subject of her passion project, and her project is her passion. If she trusts you enough to sit in the audience as she paints the picture of its process and progress, how could she allow herself to do so with a watered down delivery? Reed hates water paintings.</p>
      <p>They’re always so beautiful. But don’t you wonder how one might look without its vibrancy being washed away?</p>
      <p>Reed had woken up that day knowing for certain that she would lay out every detail to Satiya. After what happened the day before, Reed was feeling her sanguine-self drip just a bit.</p>
      <p>If this feeling sticks, I will find myself hung in MoMA!</p>
      <p>The performance was triggered when Satiya asked Reed to be her plus-one at her cousin’s funeral. “The main appetizer will be a service followed with an entree during the train ride, where we will arrive at the desert for dessert.”</p>
      <p>What a strange sequence of events for a funeral.</p>
      <p>As though through telekineses, Satiya responded to Reed’s thought, “I don’t know when they became so celebratory, but we’ll be staying in an up-and-coming ghost town for the weekend. Everyone will have their own room, and I’ve never been to a desert before let alone spent a weekend with the ghosts…”</p>
      <p>Reed realized that she herself had also yet to experience such a setting.</p>
      <p>“Would you like to go to the desert with me Reed?”</p>
      <p>Reed’s brain told Reed that she would in fact like to, but the thought lost itself on the way to her tongue.</p>
      <p>“My project is finally being birthed,” said Reed. “After six years of research, I can’t bear the thought of putting my brain in a space filled with the dead…”</p>
      <p>Satiya hmm’d.</p>
      <p>“Think of all the movies you seen about possessed babies; I don’t want to preform an exorcist now that it’s finally here.”</p>
      <p>Satiya mmm’d.</p>
      <p>“Although…” Reed in that moment realized this was indeed the perfect moment to flow into the pitch. “I suppose this final form is in a way nothing but a mutation of its past versions…” Reed stopped to think about the proposal again, but the thought was cut short by Satiya’s sixth sense. “No, that’s okay. A project is a priority…”</p>
      <p>Although Reed hadn’t formally begun its formal formation until only six months before that ramble, she formulaically computed its logistics for its five and a half years post-conception.</p>
      <p>“It’s brought me through quite the mental journey,” she told Reed. “Some of those earlier, mutated forms really dragged me through parts of my brain I hadn’t really been interested in; and yet, I’ve mind-mapped through those spaces well-enough to consider myself an expert across two dozen domains.”</p>
      <p>This led Satiya to begin her own mind-mapping. Since Satiya had never been around for Reed’s research sessions, she had always been confused on how Reed fell into work in the ways that Reed did. Even though Reed had always liked to read, she’d never taken interest in standing among the stems. Especially not after learning that painters see bushes, plants, and trees as being the gateway drug for watercolors.</p>
      <p>About six months into her project, Reed called Satiya as she did anytime she was experiencing burnout from her project. Of course, Reed wouldn’t discuss her project itself; instead, she would talk endlessly of the highs and lows that came with each new job. That particular night, Reed was explaining how exciting it is to finally be able to work with her hands in her new position with the LED manufacturing plant’s product engineering team.</p>
      <p>Surprised was Satiya because the fluorescent building was a seventy-minute train ride from Reed’s apartment. What a dreadful commute. Only a second after thinking about that cramped train pacing down a track buried half a mile below the ground, Surprised-Satiya fell into bed with Confused-Satiya. When the fuck did you become an engineer?</p>
      <p>The time Reed spent building the prototype for a wireless electronic device taught her that if you were to hold a dense synthetic like memory foam for long enough, it can become quite warm. If you were to grip even harder, then maybe it would become so warm that it may act as the sole power source for the device’s internal energy system. Maybe if the device were to be embedded within such a material, then the foam could act as a supplemental sensory element. By using this grip-able exterior to compliment the push buttons and haptic feedback sources that had already mapped out in the six variations of blueprints on Reed’s laptop, then the electronic device can function as a universal remote, and a stress relieving, multi-sensorial toy.</p>
      <p>Have you ever been so frustrated by the poorly-mixed movies you watch in your home that you want to throw your remote across the room each time you are forced to grab it to adjust the volume?</p>
      <p>The pitch for her million dollar business was the only consistent attribute at the top of those first six blueprints. Yet once she learned that such conductive powering could not be achieved, at least not with her “bare resources,” she figured that her project’s main objective of producing a product worthy of global dissemination would probably be best if applied to something more doable.</p>
      <p>“I can’t afford an engineer right now! Especially not after the costs that came with all those supplies.” She shrugged into the air she’d been walking into so hard that it added some resistance for several feet in front of her. This made her body drag, a motion that would prove handy momentarily.</p>
      <p>“So now I have to figure out what to do with the three queen-sized memory foam mattress toppers and the 24 sets of push-buttons I found myself in possession of. Do you know how much come in a set?”</p>
      <p>Satiya, of course, had no idea. Her head shook only once before Reed matched the game of visual conversation initiated by Satiya by putting every one of her ten fingers up. This meant that the energy drink Reed had been holding with her left hand had its lip bitten down by Reed’s teeth. She didn’t close her lips over the can’s, of course; that would be inappropriate, even if it were to only have been a three second kiss.</p>
      <p>After those three seconds of no-contact, Reed gripped the can away from her mouth back to the warmth of her hand in order to continue on dragging through her resistant breath that only got heavier as she sped through her speech. If the audience of her pitch were to become disengaged for even a second, then how would she bring them back into the atmosphere being constructed from her words?</p>
      <p>“I fear that 240 buttons may be too far from relevancy for the project’s current and final form. How could you work memory foam and buttons into—”</p>
      <p>And then arrived Satiya’s shout. So the smog, thicker than ever, broke right as Reed was introducing the finale. Luckily, for Reed, the break came only after its assistance in softening Reed’s abrupt halt. Satiya had interrupted Reed’s story with only two feet of space between them and the thing on the ground being pointed at with the tip of Satiya’s middle finger. It was her longest finger, and she always made sure to use that when directing someone’s attention towards something. While the thing being pointed at was still a blur, at least Satiya’s middle finger made very clear where that blur sat.</p>
      <p>It was a trick Satiya picked up when she was nineteen. In the six years since her birthday celebration, that middle finger became her best friend. Since she had just graduated high school, and had very adamantly rejected the notion of attending college, she spent a lot of time around people who she’d always known. People who’d always expected her to be elsewhere because of her brainpower.</p>
      <p>Her parents, her younger sister, the worker in the corner store below her family’s apartment building, and three of her friends in the grade below her had all been vocal about their surprise to her adamancy for not pursuing higher-ed.</p>
      <p>“There’s just so much to be appreciated in the world that’s around us. Why should I spend all that time with nothing to observe other than backpacks and lecturers? No, I’m far too grateful for everything else.”</p>
      <p>By constructing her argument around a desire to become the world’s most famed observer, she was forced to be on the lookout for everything in all scenarios. Adi, the man at the corner store, told her that arriving at such a position could never happen. “If you are the one looking, then who would be looking at you? Who would you be famous to if all of it is for you?”</p>
      <p>Adi’s rebuttal made Satiya squint in her thoughts and look around. She wasn’t usually interested in talking about ideas. It was much better to keep them to herself, only speaking about her observations. She always made sure to speak about her observations. In thinking this, Satiya realized this thought alone was sufficient to counter Adi’s argument. She felt relieved in knowing it could still be possible. She kept that thought to herself.</p>
      <p>However, perhaps as the result of telekinesis that Adi had also been keeping to himself, he gave a second consideration. “Besides,” he began after turning his attention from Satiya to the customer who’d just entered the store, “how would you get there without even being able to see yourself?”</p>
      <p>Satiya had also turned toward the customer during Adi’s delivery. After thinking about what he’d said, Satiya decided to walk out of the store without turning back toward Adi. She couldn’t let him hear the thoughts in her head; they were too muddled with the realization that he was correct.</p>
      <p>She spent that night sitting in her room on her own memory foam mattress which was pressed against the apartment’s glass corner. It was always too hot at night for her to fall asleep comfortably, and it was that view from her room, facing the intersection above the corner store, that gave her all the observation-related training she’d needed since her family moved into the apartment on her ninth birthday.</p>
      <p>What would typically have been an ideal night for watching was instead a blurred and smudged by Satiya’s salty eyes. The people she’d watched to distort her vision so much that her own reflection was itself a smudge. Not only had her perception become wonky from Adi being right that night, but her body was bound to her apartment by the knots she herself had tied tighter with each decibel of vocalization in her consistent rejection of college. How could she unravel herself into the admittance of being wrong? She couldn’t.</p>
      <p>Catching sight of her empty and dissociated gaze out onto the street exactly where he had been standing, a man in some outfit of some color that couldn’t be made out stuck up a finger in Satiya’s direction. While he himself could not be any more smudged, that stuck up finger from the stuck up person was long enough for her to catch and throw right back at him.</p>
      <p>Just as clear as he was became everything in her line of sight. The world became a fog that only she could see.</p>
